# Shell code stays LF in the repository AND in every checkout.
#
# docker build copies the working tree into the build context, so a Windows
# checkout with core.autocrlf=true would hand bash a CRLF entrypoint, and the
# container would fail with a shebang it cannot execute. install.sh is LF for
# the same reason: it is run by bash on Linux, Git Bash and Termux alike.
*.sh text eol=lf
docker/* text eol=lf

# PowerShell keeps the checkout's native decision (a BOM matters, the EOL does not),
# and cmd/vbs launchers are happier with CRLF.
*.vbs text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
